      en: "The Casa de la Moneda in Potosí is one of the most monumental and historically significant structures from the Spanish colonial era in all of South America. Within this massive, fortress-like complex, silver harvested from the legendary Cerro Rico was transformed into the currency that fueled the global Spanish Empire. Spanning an entire city block, the building remains a masterpiece of colonial architecture and engineering. Today, it serves as Bolivia's premier historical museum, where visitors can still see the original massive wooden minting machines powered by teams of mules. A tour of its galleries provides a profound understanding of the immense economic influence Potosí wielded during the 17th and 18th centuries."
