      en: "The Casa de la Libertad in Sucre is regarded as the hallowed cradle of the Bolivian nation, as it was here that the Declaration of Independence was signed in 1825. Originally part of the Jesuit University of San Francisco Xavier, the building provided the meeting hall for the founding congress. The interior remains deeply atmospheric, preserving the solemnity of the republic's birth with period furniture and original portraits of the 'Libertadores' Simón Bolívar and Antonio José de Sucre. The grand assembly hall is particularly noted for its exquisite Mudéjar-style carved wood ceiling. Today, it functions as a premier museum housing the nation's most vital historical documents."
